id: pm-qqoumq
title: "GH-1032: extension manifests silently accept unenforced version-floor spellings"
description: "Extension compatibility tooling ignores unknown or naturally misplaced manifest keys, allowing packages to declare apparent pm version floors that no runtime or author-time check reads."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[7]: agent-ux,compatibility,extensions,github-1032,manifest,sdk,validation
created_at: "2026-08-15T15:26:48.504Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T15:29:21.230Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 360
acceptance_criteria: "Define the authoritative manifest key schema in the SDK; detect unknown top-level and compatibility-like nested keys without rejecting legitimate extension namespaces; add typed findings and exact migration targets; apply the same analyzer to scaffolding, package doctor, CLI, MCP, contracts, and first-party package governance; test the three reported spellings plus negative controls in disposable packages; document compatibility and rollout behavior."
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make extension compatibility declarations schema-closed and impossible to mistake for enforced policy.
value: "Package authors receive deterministic diagnostics for misspelled, misplaced, stale, or unsupported compatibility declarations."
impact: "Packages can remain green while shipping a version floor that no consumer enforces, causing fleet drift and downstream history incompatibility."
outcome: "SDK analyzers, manifests, scaffolds, package doctor, CLI, MCP, and contracts share one strict compatibility-key vocabulary with migration guidance."
why_now: GitHub #1032 documents three live packages with three spellings and silent green checks.
parent: pm-doxj
risk: high
confidence: high
severity: high
environment: Current extension manifest SDK and package fleet on 2026-08-15
repro_steps: "Pass manifests containing compatibility.pm, nested pm.pm_min_version, and stale top-level pm_min_version through checkExtensionManifestCompatibility; observe no unknown-key finding and compatible=true for the first two."
expected_result: Unknown or misplaced compatibility-like keys are diagnosed with a stable typed finding and an executable correction; valid top-level pm_min_version remains enforced across SDK and package surfaces.
actual_result: "Compatibility-looking fields can be ignored silently, so internally consistent packages and tests remain green while no version floor is enforced."
